From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Casota <dcasota@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] installer: restrict sshd to FIPS-approved algorithms

Enabling FIPS makes the installed system unreachable over SSH.

_setup_security() adds fips=1 to the kernel command line and installs
openssl-fips-provider, but leaves sshd with its stock algorithm lists.
Those include curve25519, chacha20-poly1305, ed25519 and umac, none of
which are FIPS-approved. sshd advertises them anyway, the peer selects
one, and the FIPS-constrained crypto then refuses the operation:

  sshd-session: ssh_dispatch_run_fatal: Connection from 192.168.225.1
      port 52972: invalid argument [preauth]

The connection dies after key exchange. Nothing reports a fault - the
system is "running", there are no failed units, and sshd is active and
listening on 22 - so the machine looks healthy while remote
administration is impossible. Reproduced on a kickstart install with
security.fips true and no STIG hardening: a default client fails, and
the same client restricted to approved algorithms connects immediately.

Two existing mechanisms would have prevented this and neither applies
here. The STIG role sets Ciphers and MACs itself, and is explicit about
why ("This must be before we install the OpenSSL FIPS Provider or the
default OpenSSH Ciphers will cause issues with connecting over SSH"),
but FIPS can be enabled without STIG. The openssh package carries a
hardened sshd_config with approved algorithms, but only when it is built
with STIG_HARDEN, which defaults to 0.

So the installer has to close it: it is what puts the system into FIPS
mode, and it cannot assume which openssh build variant is on the media.

Existing active settings for these keywords are removed rather than
appended after, because sshd uses the first value it sees for them.
Commented examples are left untouched.

Signed-off-by: Daniel Casota <dcasota@gmail.com>
---
--- a/photon_installer/installer.py
+++ b/photon_installer/installer.py
@@ -1698,6 +1698,62 @@
         if self.install_config.get('eject_cdrom', True):
             self.cmd.run(['eject', '-r'])
 
+    # FIPS 140-3 approved SSH algorithms. sshd's stock lists include
+    # curve25519, chacha20-poly1305, ed25519 and umac, none of which are
+    # approved. With fips=1 sshd still advertises them, the peer selects one,
+    # and the FIPS-constrained crypto then refuses the operation:
+    #
+    #   sshd-session: ssh_dispatch_run_fatal: Connection from <ip>:
+    #       invalid argument [preauth]
+    #
+    # The connection dies after key exchange, so remote administration is
+    # impossible even though the system reports itself healthy - no failed
+    # units, sshd active and listening.
+    FIPS_SSHD_ALGORITHMS = {
+        'KexAlgorithms': 'ecdh-sha2-nistp256,ecdh-sha2-nistp384,'
+                         'ecdh-sha2-nistp521,diffie-hellman-group16-sha512,'
+                         'diffie-hellman-group18-sha512,'
+                         'diffie-hellman-group14-sha256',
+        'Ciphers': 'aes256-gcm@openssh.com,aes128-gcm@openssh.com,'
+                   'aes256-ctr,aes192-ctr,aes128-ctr',
+        'MACs': 'hmac-sha2-512-etm@openssh.com,hmac-sha2-256-etm@openssh.com,'
+                'hmac-sha2-512,hmac-sha2-256',
+        'HostKeyAlgorithms': 'rsa-sha2-512,rsa-sha2-256,ecdsa-sha2-nistp256,'
+                             'ecdsa-sha2-nistp384,ecdsa-sha2-nistp521',
+        'PubkeyAcceptedAlgorithms': 'rsa-sha2-512,rsa-sha2-256,'
+                                    'ecdsa-sha2-nistp256,ecdsa-sha2-nistp384,'
+                                    'ecdsa-sha2-nistp521',
+    }
+
+    def _restrict_sshd_to_fips_algorithms(self):
+        sshd_config = os.path.join(self.photon_root, "etc/ssh/sshd_config")
+        if not os.path.exists(sshd_config):
+            return
+
+        # sshd uses the FIRST value it sees for these keywords, so an existing
+        # setting would win over anything appended. Drop the active ones and
+        # write the approved values instead; commented examples are left alone.
+        lowered = {k.lower() for k in self.FIPS_SSHD_ALGORITHMS}
+        with open(sshd_config, "rt") as fin:
+            lines = fin.readlines()
+
+        kept = []
+        for line in lines:
+            head = line.strip().split(' ')[0].split('\t')[0].lower()
+            if head in lowered:
+                continue
+            kept.append(line)
+
+        if kept and not kept[-1].endswith("\n"):
+            kept.append("\n")
+        kept.append("\n# FIPS 140-3: offer only approved algorithms.\n")
+        for key in sorted(self.FIPS_SSHD_ALGORITHMS):
+            kept.append(f"{key} {self.FIPS_SSHD_ALGORITHMS[key]}\n")
+
+        with open(sshd_config, "wt") as fout:
+            fout.writelines(kept)
+        self.logger.info("restricted sshd algorithms to the FIPS-approved set")
+
     def _setup_security(self):
         if 'security' not in self.install_config:
             return
@@ -1707,6 +1763,7 @@
             self.poi_kernel_cmdline += " security=selinux selinux=1"
         if security.get('fips', False):
             self.poi_kernel_cmdline += " fips=1"
+            self._restrict_sshd_to_fips_algorithms()
 
         selinux = security.get('selinux', None)
         if selinux is not None:
